  "documentation": {
    "range": "Within the European region, this species is widespread across the continent to 66° N, with vagrants seen far northwards. It is found at altitudes between sea level and 900 m.<br/><br/>In the EU 27 region, this species' area of occupancy is 175,616 km² and the extent of occurrence is 11,618,934 km².<br/><br/>The global range of this species extends across the Palearctic. The global distribution area of the species is situated both within and outside Europe.",
    "population": "This is a common and widespread species in its European range. Declines in distribution or population have been reported in Bosnia and Herzegovina and Romania where it is classed as Near Threatened (Maes <em>et al. </em>2019).",
    "habitats": "The Large White can be found in almost all habitat types. It lays its eggs singly or in small clusters on the underside of the leaves of the larval hostplants, which are mainly broad-leaved crucifers, like <em>Brassica</em> spp., <em>Crambe</em> spp., <em>Lunaria</em> spp. and <em>Cakile</em> spp., including various cultivated brassicas, that offer enough food for the gregarious caterpillars. On such a nutritious diet, the caterpillar grows very quickly, reaching the pupal stage in three to six weeks. The white pupa is suspended in a silken girdle from the foodplant. The Large White has several generations a year, the number depending on the geographical location and latitude of the flight area and the length of the summer. Habitats include urban parks and large gardens (12%), towns, villages, industrial sites (9%), orchards, groves and tree plantations (8%), tree lines, hedges, small woods, bocage, parkland dehesa (7%), mesophile grasslands (7%), improved grasslands (6%) (van Swaay <em>et al. </em>2006).",
    "threats": "This species is not believed to face major threats at the European level.",
    "measures": "This species occurs in several protected areas across its range. No specific conservation actions are needed at the European level, although populations should be monitored by a butterfly monitoring scheme.",
    "use_trade": "All butterflies are collected to some extent, but only for the extremely rare species can it be a problem and the trade in Europe is generally at a low level compared to other continents. There is no specific trade information for this species.",
    "rationale": "European regional assessment: Least Concern (LC)<br/>EU 27 regional assessment: Least Concern (LC)<br/><br/>Within the European region, the Large White is widespread across the continent. This species is listed as Least Concern, as it has been evaluated against all the IUCN criteria and does not meet the thresholds for any category of threat in Europe or the EU 27 region.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "<em>Pieris wollastoni</em> (Butler, 1886) is considered a subspecies of <em>P. brassicae</em> by some authorities, but is maintaned and assessed here as a separate species."
  },
